[[Image:Sperrklinke Schema.svg|thumb|A ratchet featuring a pawl (a) and gear (b)]]

===Etymology===
Perhaps from Low German or Dutch ''[[pal#Dutch|pal]]''.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/pɔːl/}}
*: {{rhymes|ɔːl}}
* Homophones: [[pall]], [[Paul]]

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A pivoted [[catch]] designed to fall into a [[notch]] on a [[ratchet wheel]] so as to allow movement in only one direction (e.g. on a windlass or in a clock mechanism), or alternatively to move the wheel in one direction.
#* '''1994''', Cormac McCarthy, ''The Crossing'':
#*: The nails in the rim of the wheel went ratcheting over the leather '''pawl''' and the wheel slowed and came to a stop and the woman turned to the crowd and smiled.
#* '''1910111, Victor Appleton, ''Tom Swift and his Motorcycle''
#*: A pawl is .

